// LeetCode 275 — H-Index II.
//
// `citations` is sorted ASCENDING. Return the h-index: the largest `h` such
// that at least `h` papers have at least `h` citations each. Must be O(log n).
//
// [`#274`](../274-h-index/) is the same definition on UNSORTED input, where the
// answer costs a sort or a counting pass. Handing the array over sorted changes
// the problem entirely: the qualifying condition becomes monotone in the index,
// and monotone means binary search.
//
// THE ★ MECHANISM: BINARY SEARCH ON THE INDEX. At index `i` the papers at
// `[i, n)` are the `n - i` most-cited ones. If `citations[i] >= n - i`, then
// each of those `n - i` papers has at least `n - i` citations, so `n - i` is an
// achievable h. Larger `i` means fewer papers, so a smaller and easier target —
// the predicate `citations[i] >= n - i` is therefore FALSE for a prefix and TRUE
// for a suffix, and the answer is `n - i` at the first TRUE.
//
// That monotonicity is the whole content of the problem, and it is worth being
// precise about WHY it holds, because it is not simply "the array is sorted".
// Moving from `i` to `i + 1` raises the left side (`citations` is
// non-decreasing) and lowers the right side (`n - i` shrinks by one). Both
// movements favour the predicate, so once it is true it stays true. A predicate
// where only one side moved would not be searchable this way.
//
// NO QUALIFYING INDEX MEANS h = 0 — the all-uncited case — which falls out of
// the search naturally if the low bound is allowed to run to `n`.
//
// THE OFF-BY-ONE LIVES IN THE HALF-OPEN INVARIANT, not in the comparison.
// `lo` is a candidate; `hi` is one past the last candidate. `citations[mid] >=
// n - mid` means `mid` qualifies, so the answer is at `mid` or LEFT of it and
// `hi = mid`; otherwise it is strictly right and `lo = mid + 1`. Writing
// `hi = mid - 1` here is the classic way to skip the answer, and it is wrong on
// exactly the inputs where the first qualifying index is the one being tested.

fn h_index(citations: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = citations.len();
    let mut lo = 0;
    let mut hi = n;                      // one PAST the last candidate
    while lo < hi {
        let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2; // no overflow even at i64's ceiling
        if citations[mid] >= n - mid {
            hi = mid;                    // `mid` qualifies — it or something left of it
        } else {
            lo = mid + 1;             // strictly right
        }
    }
    return n - lo;                       // lo == n when nothing qualifies -> 0
}

fn show(xs: ref Vec[i64]) -> String {
    let mut s = "[";
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < xs.len() {
        if i > 0 { s = s + ","; }
        s = s + f"{xs[i]}";
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return s + "]";
}

fn main() {
    let cases: Vec[Vec[i64]] = [
        [0, 1, 3, 5, 6],   // 3
        [1, 2, 100],             // 2
        [0],                           // 0
        [100],                         // 1
        [0, 0, 0],               // 0  — nothing is cited
        [5, 5, 5],               // 3  — everything clears the bar
        [0, 0, 4, 4],         // 2
        [1, 1, 1, 1, 1],   // 1  — a wide flat plateau
        [0, 1, 2, 3, 4],   // 2  — the diagonal crossing
    ];
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < cases.len() {
        println(f"{show(cases[i])} -> {h_index(cases[i])}");
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
